  "content": "{\"key_points\":[\"A computer fundamentally performs calculations and stores results in memory; modern computers are very fast (a billion calculations per second is probably not far off) and commonly have hundreds of gigabytes of storage. Calculations are of two kinds: primitive calculations built into the language (addition, subtraction, multiplication, etc.) and new calculations programmers define by combining those primitives. Computers only know what you tell them; they are not magical and have no mind.\",\"Declarative knowledge is statements of fact, while imperative knowledge is the recipe, how-to, or sequence of steps. Ana Bell illustrated this with a raffle: the statement of fact was that someone would win a prize (a Google Cardboard) before class ended, and the imperative steps were to open the Anaconda IDE, choose a random number between the first and nth responder, and find that number in the responder sheet. Using Python's random.randint between 16 and 272, the number 75 was chosen, corresponding to Lauren Zov.\",\"A numerical example contrasts declarative and imperative knowledge: declarative knowledge is that the square root of x is y such that y times y equals x, which computers do not know what to do with; imperative knowledge is the algorithm for finding the square root of x. Starting with a guess g (e.g., 3 for x = 16), calculate g times g (9); if g times g is close enough to x, stop and say g is the answer; if not, make a new guess by averaging g and x over g, then repeat the process with the new guess until close enough.\",\"The recipe for finding the square root of x has three parts: a simple sequence of steps (four steps), flow of control (decisions such as whether we are close enough, and repetition of steps 1, 2, 3), and a way to stop (in this example, deciding we are close enough, perhaps within .01 or .001). In computer science, this recipe is an algorithm.\",\"Historically there were fixed-program computers, such as a calculator that only knows addition, multiplication, subtraction, and division and cannot plot, go on the internet, or send email; creating a machine to do another thing required creating another fixed-program computer. Stored-program computers can store a sequence of instructions, execute it, and change the sequence to execute different instructions, doing different tasks in the same machine.\",\"The basic machine architecture has four main parts: memory (containing data and the sequence of instructions), input/output, the ALU (which performs primitive operations such as addition and subtraction), and the Control Unit, which contains one program counter. The program counter starts at the first instruction, gets the instruction, and sends it to the ALU; the ALU may get data from memory, do operations, and store data back into memory, after which the program counter increases by 1 to go to the next instruction. A test instruction (e.g., is a value greater or equal to or the same as another value) returns true or false, and depending on the result the program may go to the next instruction or set the program counter back to the beginning, so execution is not always linear.\",\"The primitive operations a stored-program computer can do are addition, subtraction, logic operations, tests (something equal to something else, something less than, etc.), and moving data (storing and moving data around). The interpreter goes through every instruction and decides whether to go to the next instruction, skip instructions, or repeat instructions.\",\"Alan Turing showed that you can compute anything using six primitives: move left, move right, read, write, scan, and do nothing. Using those six instructions and a piece of tape, programming languages came about that created a more convenient set of primitives. An important consequence is that if you can compute something in Python, then in theory you can write a program that computes the exact same thing in any other language.\",\"Once you have your set of primitives for a language, you can create expressions, which are combinations of the primitives and have some value and meaning in the programming language. In English, primitive constructs are words; in Python, primitives include floats, Booleans, numbers, strings, and simple operators like addition and subtraction.\",\"Syntax and static semantics parallel English: in English, \\\"cat, dog, boy\\\" is not syntactically valid (noun, noun, noun), while \\\"cat, hugs boy\\\" (noun, verb, noun) is syntactically valid; in Python, a word followed by the number five is not syntactically valid, but operator, operand, operator is OK. For static semantics, \\\"I are hungry\\\" has good syntax but is weird (pronoun, verb, adjective), while \\\"I am hungry\\\" is better; similarly, programming languages have static semantics.\",\"The course 6.0001/600 has three big goals: knowledge of concepts (taught through lectures and tested by exams), programming skills, and problem solving (taught through the psets), all underpinned by practice. The material divides into three sections: learning how to program (what objects to create, representing knowledge with data structures, and control flow since programs are not just linear), writing good, readable, well-organized, modular code that others and your future self can understand, and the computer science part of comparing programs, algorithms, and efficiency.\"],\"summary\":\"This first lecture of 6.0001/600 introduces what computation is and begins Python basics. It frames the course around three goals—concepts, programming skills, and problem solving through psets—and three sections: learning to program (data structures and control flow), writing good, readable, modular code, and comparing programs and algorithms for efficiency. It explains that computers perform calculations and store results, only doing what they are told, and distinguishes declarative knowledge (statements of fact) from imperative knowledge (recipes or sequences of steps), using a raffle and a square-root algorithm as examples. It then covers the history from fixed-program to stored-program computers, the basic machine architecture (memory, input/output, ALU, and Control Unit with a program counter), primitive operations, Turing's six primitives, and the parallel between English and programming languages in syntax and static semantics.\"}",
